  "candidate_text": "Writing that carries a voice is writing that keeps its promises. You tell the reader what changed, and the reader can check it. The plan was written for you: every claim sits next to the fact that backs it. Really short sentences carry weight; actually, they carry the most weight when the facts are heavy. We were told the opposite, but the record says otherwise--the metrics were never invented here. A promise kept quietly (the plain verb, the plain noun) is a promise kept twice. Why say the thing again? Because the reader was listening the first time, and the reader will remember it -- that is the whole point. Keep the copy honest; keep it short; let the reader finish it.",
